      "question_text": "Members of a species typically all have the same number of chromosomes.\n(a) \nExplain the need for both fusion of gametes and meiosis in a sexual life cycle.\b\n[4]\n(b) \nDescribe the events in interphase and mitosis that ensure that the chromosome \nnumber does not change during an asexual life cycle.\b\n[7]\n(c) \nDiscuss the barriers to hybridization between species and how polyploidy can \novercome these barriers.\b\n[4]",
      "answer_text": "c \na. courtship/sexual behaviour prevents interbreeding/hybridization\nOR\nspecies do not usually mate with other species;\nb. temporal isolation (in reproduction)/mating season differences prevent interbreeding/hybridization;\nc.\ngeographical/physical isolation/separation may prevent interbreeding/hybridization;\nd. differences in reproductive anatomy can prevent interbreeding/hybridization / example of this;\ne. (interspecific) hybrids are (usually) sterile/infertile/cannot produce (viable) offspring;\nf.\nmeiosis fails (in interspecific hybrids);\ng. differences in chromosome number/structure;\nh. so chromosomes cannot pair/are not homologous/bivalents not formed;\ni.\npolyploidy is doubling of chromosome number/having more sets of chromosomes (than normal);\nj.\nsterile interspecific hybrids can become fertile if the chromosome number is doubled/by polyploidy;\nk.\ntetraploids produce diploid gametes;\nl.\nnew species can be formed by polyploidy in interspecific hybrids;\na. For the second\nalternative, do not accept\ninterbreeding instead of\nmating.\ni. Do not award for\nanswers describing\naneuploidy or non-\ndisjunction of individual\nbivalents.\n4 \nmax \n\n– 17 –\n2225 – 6021 \nQuestion \nAnswers \nNotes \nTotal",
