      "question_text": "Defects in the formation of synapses could be the cause of neurological disorders such as \nAlzheimer’s disease that affects the ability to think and remember clearly. It is more frequent \nin people older than 65 years of age. The graph shows the changes in synapse number \nover time. The diagram shows activity-related neural development.\nSynapse number\nSynapse \nformation\nChildhood\nAdulthood\nAdolescence\nSynapse \nelimination\nSynapse \nmaintenance\n \nBirth\nTime\nStrong mental\nactivity\nWeak mental\nactivity\nStabilized \nsynapses\nPruned synapse\nExcitatory neuron\n[Source: adapted from Doll, C. A. and Broadie, K. (2014), Impaired activity-dependent neural circuit assembly and refinement \n in autism spectrum disorder genetic models. Frontiers in Cellular Neuroscience 8: 30. \ndoi: 10.3389/fncel.2014.00030. Copyright © 2014 Doll and Broadie.]\n(Option A continues on the following page)\n\n36EP09\n– 9 –\nTurn over\nN16/4/BIOLO/HP3/ENG/TZ0/XX\n(Option A, question 4 continued)\n \n(a) \nState what happens to unused neurons.\n[1]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n(b) \nPredict how mental activity might delay the onset of Alzheimer’s disease.\n[2]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n(c) \nAutism appears early in life and affects how a person communicates and relates to \nothers. There is evidence that autism could be caused by a surplus of synapses. \nUsing all of the information provided, suggest two possible causes of a surplus of \nsynapses in people with autism.\n[2]\n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . \n(Option A continues on the following page)\n\n36EP10\n– 10 –\nN16/4/BIOLO/HP3/ENG/TZ0/XX\n(Option A continued)",
